WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.640
So we're on to our next section regarding Composition API

00:03.640 --> 00:04.680
with Script Setup.

00:08.040 --> 00:11.040
All right, so let's context set a bit here.

00:11.040 --> 00:13.240
When we take a look at this user page,

00:13.240 --> 00:16.960
you'll notice that, well, most of our component

00:16.960 --> 00:21.880
is actually in Composition API, like minus the components

00:21.880 --> 00:23.000
registration right here.

00:23.000 --> 00:25.040
Everything else is Composition API.

00:25.040 --> 00:27.000
And so Vue was certainly, when we were looking,

00:27.000 --> 00:29.040
thinking about the developer experience of working

00:29.040 --> 00:31.440
in Composition API, it was a bit like, well, OK,

00:31.440 --> 00:32.240
this is cool, right?

00:32.240 --> 00:33.920
We can break stuff up.

00:33.920 --> 00:35.760
We can go ahead and write our JavaScript in a way

00:35.760 --> 00:37.160
that's kind of more natural for us.

00:37.160 --> 00:38.600
We can group it the way we want.

00:38.600 --> 00:42.320
But then the fact that we have to constantly write our returns

00:42.320 --> 00:43.840
gets a little bit tedious, especially when

00:43.840 --> 00:45.200
you have a lot of things to return.

00:45.200 --> 00:47.160
That's not something you have to manage in two different places.

00:47.160 --> 00:48.680
You have to define what you're going to do,

00:48.680 --> 00:49.920
then you're going to return it.

00:49.920 --> 00:53.200
Couldn't Vue be a little bit smarter about this somehow?

00:53.200 --> 00:55.840
And so this is where things like build tools are really great.

00:55.840 --> 01:01.000
Because with Vue 3.2, we introduced a compiler optimization

01:01.000 --> 01:04.360
where it allows us to actually fully buy into the Composition

01:04.360 --> 01:07.760
API while saving us some code space and, more importantly,

01:07.760 --> 01:10.480
allowing us to be more succinct and concise.

01:10.480 --> 01:12.400
And so let me show you what I mean by that.

